  "mouthSideLabelling": {
    "method": "the height of the surface each vertex grows out of, against the measured mouth line",
    "jawSideVertices": 578,
    "skullSideVertices": 9117,
    "note": "a height test on the vertex itself splits interlocking teeth down the middle and a graded one stretches each half between two bones -- which is how this fish's upper tooth row first came out as a comb of needles two head-depths long. Taking the height of the smoothed surface instead, which is the tooth's own base, puts every tooth whole on the jaw it grows from. Flood-filling labels from the skin was tried first and is worse on a needle rostrum: the seeds are sparse on a tube that thin and the label boundary wanders through it, which tore the snout into ribbons."
  },
